\newlabel{sec:law}{{5}{4}{A single law across profile shapes}{section.5}{}}
\newlabel{eq:mix}{{3}{4}{A single law across profile shapes}{equation.5.3}{}}
\newlabel{eq:law}{{4}{4}{A single law across profile shapes}{equation.5.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {(a)} Blur preference $\Lambda $ against retained spectral power $u$ at $t=0.002$, mean\nobreakspace  {}$\pm $\nobreakspace  {}s.d.\ over 120 images, for four representative kernels (colour, markers with error bars) and four further kernels (grey). The dashed line is the measured estimator noise floor, $\mathrm  {sd}(\Lambda )=388$ nats. \textbf  {(b)} Radially binned power spectra $\langle |\hat  h|^2\rangle $ of the four coloured kernels: the shapes are genuinely very different, which is what makes the near-coincidence in (a) a result rather than a tautology. At matched $u$ the eight kernels agree to within $1.42$--$1.77\times $.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:phen}{{1}{5}{\textbf {(a)} Blur preference $\Lam $ against retained spectral power $u$ at $t=0.002$, mean~$\pm $~s.d.\ over 120 images, for four representative kernels (colour, markers with error bars) and four further kernels (grey). The dashed line is the measured estimator noise floor, $\mathrm {sd}(\Lam )=388$ nats. \textbf {(b)} Radially binned power spectra $\langle |\hat h|^2\rangle $ of the four coloured kernels: the shapes are genuinely very different, which is what makes the near-coincidence in (a) a result rather than a tautology. At matched $u$ the eight kernels agree to within $1.42$--$1.77\times $.\relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
\citation{nguyen2025morsebott}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {(a)} The prefix $C(u)=\Lambda (u)\,u^{-b(u)}$ with $b(u)$ the exponent of panel\nobreakspace  {}(b), normalised so that the geometric mean of the seven smooth blur kernels is $1$ at each $u$; the shaded band is their min--max. The frozen-exponent definition is used only to compare operators \emph  {at fixed $u$}; it is not the law of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:law}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}, whose $u$-dependence is the integrated form. Four profile families that had not been measured before the test, plus a fifth kept as an internal control against the real library kernel (colour), all fall \emph  {inside} that band, to within $1.13\times $ of its mean. The one profile that escapes is the ideal low-pass with a hard cut, whose attenuation has no passband. \textbf  {(b)} The local exponent $b(u)=\mathrm  {d}\qopname  \relax o{log}\Lambda /\mathrm  {d}\qopname  \relax o{log}u$ measured on the three $u$ segments: the seven-kernel mean with its min--max band, the five new families, and the closed form $b(u)\approx -0.70-6.4\,u^{3.1}$. All five new families land on the same exponent.\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:law}{{2}{6}{\textbf {(a)} The prefix $C(u)=\Lambda (u)\,u^{-b(u)}$ with $b(u)$ the exponent of panel~(b), normalised so that the geometric mean of the seven smooth blur kernels is $1$ at each $u$; the shaded band is their min--max. The frozen-exponent definition is used only to compare operators \emph {at fixed $u$}; it is not the law of Eq.~\eqref {eq:law}, whose $u$-dependence is the integrated form. Four profile families that had not been measured before the test, plus a fifth kept as an internal control against the real library kernel (colour), all fall \emph {inside} that band, to within $1.13\times $ of its mean. The one profile that escapes is the ideal low-pass with a hard cut, whose attenuation has no passband. \textbf {(b)} The local exponent $b(u)=\mathrm {d}\log \Lambda /\mathrm {d}\log u$ measured on the three $u$ segments: the seven-kernel mean with its min--max band, the five new families, and the closed form $b(u)\approx -0.70-6.4\,u^{3.1}$. All five new families land on the same exponent.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
